format: meaning/draft-1
id: software
name: Software
description: >-
  What is built and run: applications and their parts, versions and builds,
  environments, interfaces, source, accounts and logs.
license: CC0-1.0
concepts:
  - id: software-application
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: Software application
    synonyms:
      en: [application, app]
    description: >-
      Software that people run. It may be offered outside the organization
      or used only inside the organization that builds it. It is not a
      product in this graph. A product is a type of good offered for supply.
      It is not a software component, which is a part of an application, and
      it is not one version, build, release, or deployment of the
      application. It is not the service users are promised. The service is
      the capability. An application may be what provides it.

  - id: software-component
    kind: entity
    of: software-application
    labels:
      en: Software component
    description: >-
      A part of a software application, such as a module, a library or a
      microservice that is deployed on its own. Calling a part a
      microservice does not make it the service concept: a service is a
      capability offered to another party, and a component is a piece of
      software. A failing component may cause an incident on a service. The
      component is still not that service, and it is not the application
      taken as a whole.

  - id: software-component-application
    kind: attribute
    of: software-component
    labels:
      en: Software component application
    description: An application this component is a part of.
    values-of: software-application

  - id: software-component-owner-team
    kind: attribute
    of: software-component
    labels:
      en: Software component owner team
    description: A team accountable for this component.
    values-of: team

  - id: software-version
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: Software version
    synonyms:
      en: [application version]
    description: >-
      An identified revision of a software application. A release uses a
      version as the name of what it makes available. A version is not a
      build. A build is one artifact produced from a commit, and building
      the same sources again can produce another artifact of the same
      version. A version is not a deployment and not an environment. The
      version number may follow semantic versioning or another scheme; the
      scheme is not part of the identity of this concept.

  - id: software-version-number
    kind: attribute
    of: software-version
    labels:
      en: Software version number
    synonyms:
      en: [version number]
    description: >-
      The identifier people use for this version, in whatever scheme the
      application uses. The number is not the release and not the build.

  - id: software-version-application
    kind: attribute
    of: software-version
    labels:
      en: Software version application
    description: The application this version is a revision of.
    values-of: software-application

  - id: environment
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: Environment
    synonyms:
      en: [runtime environment]
    description: >-
      A subset of infrastructure used for a particular purpose, where a
      build runs. The usual kinds are development, test, staging, and
      production. Development is where the software is changed. Test is
      where it is checked. Staging is a production-like place used to
      rehearse a release. Production is where the service's users are.
      Organizations use other names and other counts, so those four kinds
      are not a closed list. An environment is not a deployment into it and
      not a release. Software that runs in development is not, by that fact,
      running in production. The word can also mean external conditions.
      This concept is the infrastructure used for a purpose.

  - id: environment-type
    kind: attribute
    of: environment
    labels:
      en: Environment type
    synonyms:
      en: [environment kind]
    description: >-
      Which kind of place this environment is, in the organization's own
      words. Development, test, staging and production are the usual kinds.
      The attribute does not fix the vocabulary: a further kind, such as a
      sandbox, is still an environment type.

  - id: build
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: Build
    synonyms:
      en: [software build, build artifact]
    description: >-
      An artifact produced by building a commit: the bytes a deployment can
      put into an environment. A build is not a software version, though a
      team may store one identifier that is used for both, and it is not a
      release or a deployment. Two builds can exist for one version when the
      same sources are built again.

  - id: build-commit
    kind: attribute
    of: build
    labels:
      en: Build commit
    description: The commit this artifact was built from.
    values-of: commit

  - id: build-version
    kind: attribute
    of: build
    labels:
      en: Build version
    description: >-
      The software version this build is an artifact of, when the build is
      identified with a version. A build of a commit that has not been given
      a version leaves this empty.
    values-of: software-version

  - id: feature-flag
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: Feature flag
    synonyms:
      en: [feature toggle, feature switch]
    description: >-
      A switch in running software that changes behavior without another
      deployment and without another release. The flag is usually shipped
      in a deployment while it is off. Turning it on is a separate act and
      is not itself a deployment. The same deployed build can behave
      differently for different users, or in different environments. A flag
      is not a version, not a build, and not an environment.

  - id: feature-flag-application
    kind: attribute
    of: feature-flag
    labels:
      en: Feature flag application
    description: The application whose running behavior this flag changes.
    values-of: software-application

  - id: feature-flag-environment
    kind: attribute
    of: feature-flag
    labels:
      en: Feature flag environment
    description: >-
      An environment in which this flag is set, when the flag is not the
      same in every environment. The environment is where the software
      runs; the flag is not that place.
    values-of: environment

  - id: api
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: API
    synonyms:
      en: [application programming interface]
    description: >-
      An interface through which one piece of software offers operations to
      another. The API is the interface as a whole. One operation of it is
      an API endpoint. An API is not a service, though a service may be
      reached through one, and it is not a deployment of the software that
      offers it.

  - id: api-software-component
    kind: attribute
    of: api
    labels:
      en: API software component
    description: The software component that offers this interface.
    values-of: software-component

  - id: api-endpoint
    kind: entity
    of: api
    labels:
      en: API endpoint
    description: >-
      One operation of an API. Where the API uses HTTP, the operation has a
      method and a path. The endpoint is not the API as a whole and not a
      deployment.

  - id: api-endpoint-api
    kind: attribute
    of: api-endpoint
    labels:
      en: API endpoint API
    description: The API this operation belongs to.
    values-of: api

  - id: api-endpoint-method
    kind: attribute
    of: api-endpoint
    labels:
      en: API endpoint method
    description: >-
      The method of the call. For an HTTP API this is the HTTP method, such
      as GET or POST. The method is not the path, and not every API uses
      HTTP methods.

  - id: api-endpoint-path
    kind: attribute
    of: api-endpoint
    labels:
      en: API endpoint path
    synonyms:
      en: [endpoint path]
    description: >-
      The path of the operation, where the API identifies operations by a
      path. The path is not the method.

  - id: repository
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: Repository
    synonyms:
      en: [source repository, code repository, repo]
    description: >-
      A store of the source of software, holding the commits of that source.
      It is not a build, not a release and not a document, though the source
      it holds may include documents.

  - id: repository-application
    kind: attribute
    of: repository
    labels:
      en: Repository application
    description: >-
      An application whose source this repository stores. One application
      can have more than one repository.
    values-of: software-application

  - id: commit
    kind: entity
    of: repository
    labels:
      en: Commit
    synonyms:
      en: [code commit]
    description: >-
      A recorded revision of source in a repository. Lead time for changes
      is measured from a commit. A commit is not a build: the build is an
      artifact produced from a commit. It is not a pull request, which
      proposes one or more commits, and it is not a release.

  - id: commit-repository
    kind: attribute
    of: commit
    labels:
      en: Commit repository
    description: The repository that holds this commit.
    values-of: repository

  - id: log-entry
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: Log entry
    synonyms:
      en: [log record, log line]
    description: >-
      One line written by running software into its log. It is not an alert.
      An alert says a condition needs attention and may be raised because of
      log entries; the line is not that alert. It is not an incident.

  - id: log-entry-level
    kind: attribute
    of: log-entry
    labels:
      en: Log entry level
    synonyms:
      en: [log level]
    description: >-
      How the software classified this line, in the scheme that software
      uses. Common grades run from a trace or debug line, through
      information and a warning, to an error. The names and the number of
      grades are not a closed list. A log level is not incident severity and
      not bug severity: it classifies a line the software wrote, not the
      impairment of a service and not a flaw on the backlog.

  - id: log-entry-timestamp
    kind: attribute
    of: log-entry
    labels:
      en: Log entry timestamp
    description: >-
      The instant the line was written. It is not the instant an incident
      was opened and not the instant an alert was acknowledged.

  - id: log-entry-software-component
    kind: attribute
    of: log-entry
    labels:
      en: Log entry software component
    description: The software component that wrote the line.
    values-of: software-component

  - id: user-account
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: User account
    synonyms:
      en: [login account]
    description: >-
      An identity an application accepts when someone signs in. It is not a
      person: one person can hold several accounts, and an account can be
      recorded before it is tied to a person. It is not an employee. It is
      not a financial account.

  - id: user-account-person
    kind: attribute
    of: user-account
    labels:
      en: User account person
    description: >-
      The person this account is tied to, when that person is known. The
      account can exist without this link.
    values-of: person

  - id: user-account-application
    kind: attribute
    of: user-account
    labels:
      en: User account application
    description: The application that accepts this account.
    values-of: software-application
